The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), and 2449 (2018), and its relevant Presidential Statements,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing grave concern at the ongoing military escalation and the intensification of hostilities in north-west Syria, including Idlib governorate and surrounding areas, which have caused massive civilian casualties, extensive destruction of civilian infrastructure, and the displacement of over 500,000 people since late April 2019,

Gravely alarmed by the catastrophic and worsening humanitarian situation in Idlib, where more than three million civilians are in need of urgent humanitarian assistance and protection,

Reiterating that all parties to the conflict must comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, in particular regarding the protection of civilians and civilian objects, and deploring all attacks directed against civilians, schools, and medical facilities,

Emphasizing that counter-terrorism operations must be conducted in full compliance with obligations under international law, in particular international humanitarian law, international human rights law and international refugee law, and stressing that the presence of terrorist organizations designated by the Security Council does not negate obligations under international humanitarian law to protect civilians and civilian infrastructure,

Recalling the Memorandum on the Stabilization of the Situation in the Idlib De-escalation Area of 17 September 2018 between the Russian Federation and the Republic of Turkey, and stressing the vital importance of preserving and fully implementing the ceasefire arrangements,

Reaffirming that there is no military solution to the conflict in Syria, and underscoring its unwavering support for a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in line with resolution 2254 (2015),

1. Demands that all parties to the conflict immediately cease hostilities in Idlib governorate and surrounding areas in north-west Syria, starting at 12:00 noon Damascus time on 21 September 2019, in order to avert a further deterioration of the catastrophic humanitarian situation;

2. Decides that the cessation of hostilities shall not apply to military operations directed against the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL, also known as Da’esh), Al-Qaida, and Hayat Tahrir al-Sham (formerly known as the Al-Nusra Front), and all other individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with Al-Qaida or ISIL, and other terrorist groups as designated by the Security Council, while underscoring that all measures taken to combat terrorism must strictly adhere to international humanitarian law;

3. Calls upon all parties to immediately cease attacks directed against civilians and civilian infrastructure, including medical facilities, medical personnel and their means of transport, and schools, in full compliance with international humanitarian law and relevant Security Council resolutions;

4. Demands that all parties ensure safe, rapid, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access across conflict lines and international borders, enabling the United Nations and its humanitarian partners to deliver life-saving assistance to all individuals in need throughout north-west Syria;

5. Urges the parties to the 17 September 2018 Memorandum to renew their efforts to fully implement its provisions and to maintain the de-escalation framework to protect civilians and de-escalate tensions;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of the cessation of hostilities and on the humanitarian situation in north-west Syria within ten days of the adoption of this resolution and to continue reporting on a regular basis;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

